  {
   "xml_index": 1,
   "xml_name": "L02_001_Comment And Print",
   "display_name": "Comment And Print",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>In Python, we can add little notes to our code called <b>comments</b>. A comment starts with a <code>#</code> symbol, and Python completely ignores everything after the <code>#</code> on that line. Comments are just for humans reading the code - they don't affect the program's output at all.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre># This program prints a greeting\nprint(\"Good Morning\")  # this line displays the greeting</pre>\n<p>This still only prints:</p>\n<pre>Good Morning</pre>\n<p>(the comments are invisible to Python)</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Write a program that prints exactly the word <code>Hello</code>. Somewhere in your code, include at least one comment (starting with <code>#</code>) explaining what your code does. The comment will not affect your output.</p>\n<p><b>Expected Output:</b></p>\n<pre>Hello</pre>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "Hello",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 2,
   "xml_name": "L02_002_Store And Reuse An Int",
   "display_name": "Store And Reuse An Int",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>An <b>int</b> (short for \"integer\") is Python's type for whole numbers, like <code>5</code>, <code>-3</code>, or <code>0</code> (no decimal point).</p>\n<p>We can store a whole number in a <b>variable</b> - think of a variable as a labelled box that holds a value we can use later. For example:</p>\n<pre>age = 20\nprint(age)\nprint(age + 5)</pre>\n<p>This stores 20 in a variable called <code>age</code>, and then reuses that same variable twice: once to print it as-is, and once to print it after adding 5.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Read one whole number from the user and store it in a variable. First, print the value exactly as read. Then, on the next line, print the value plus 10.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b></p>\n<p>Input:</p>\n<pre>5</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>5\n15</pre>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "5",
     "expected": "5\n15",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "0",
     "expected": "0\n10",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "-3",
     "expected": "-3\n7",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 3,
   "xml_name": "L02_003_Total Marks In Three Subjects",
   "display_name": "Total Marks In Three Subjects",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's practice storing several whole numbers in clearly-named variables and combining them.</p>\n<p>Imagine a student has marks in three subjects: Physics, Chemistry, and Maths. We can store each in its own <b>int</b> variable, and then add them together to get the total.</p>\n<pre>physics = 80\nchemistry = 75\nmaths = 90\ntotal = physics + chemistry + maths\nprint(total)</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Read three whole numbers (one per line), representing marks in Physics, Chemistry, and Maths in that order. Store each in a sensibly-named variable, and print the total marks.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b></p>\n<p>Input:</p>\n<pre>80\n75\n90</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>245</pre>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "80\n75\n90",
     "expected": "245",
