  {
   "xml_index": 6,
   "xml_name": "L02_006_Store And Print A Message",
   "display_name": "Store And Print A Message",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Text in Python is called a <b>string</b>. Strings are sequences of characters, written between quotes when we type them directly, like <code>\"Hello\"</code>. When read with <code>input()</code>, the result is always a string automatically - no conversion function needed!</p>\n<pre>message = input()\nprint(\"You said: \" + message)</pre>\n<p>Here, <code>+</code> between two strings doesn't add numbers, it <b>joins (concatenates)</b> them together into one longer string.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Read a single line of text from the user and store it in a variable called <code>message</code>. Print it in the exact format <code>You said: &lt;message&gt;</code></p>\n<p><b>Example:</b></p>\n<p>Input:</p>\n<pre>Python is fun</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>You said: Python is fun</pre>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
