  {
   "xml_index": 9,
   "xml_name": "L02_009_Simple Interest Then Total",
   "display_name": "Simple Interest Then Total",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A Python program runs <strong>line by line, from top to bottom</strong> - this is called <strong>sequence</strong>. Each line can use the results calculated by earlier lines, because those variables already exist by the time we reach a later line.</p>\n<p>Let's see this in action using a two-step calculation: first we calculate the Simple Interest (SI), and then we use that result to calculate the Total Amount.</p>\n<pre>SI = (Principal * Rate * Time) / 100\nTotal Amount = Principal + SI</pre>\n<p><strong>Your task:</strong> Read the principal, rate, and time (three whole numbers, one per line, in that order). First calculate and print the simple interest. Then, using that value, calculate and print the total amount (principal + interest) on the next line.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong></p>\n<p>Input:</p>\n<pre>1000\n5\n2</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>100.0\n1100.0</pre>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "1000\n5\n2",
     "expected": "100.0\n1100.0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "5000\n10\n3",
     "expected": "1500.0\n6500.0",
     "extra": "",
