  {
   "xml_index": 10,
   "xml_name": "L02_010_Positive Or Negative",
   "display_name": "Positive Or Negative",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>So far, every program you've written has run every single line, in order, from top to bottom. But real programs need to <b>make decisions</b> too - a game needs to decide if you won or lost, a shop needs to decide if you get a discount, a login page needs to decide if your password is correct. Python lets us make these decisions using <code>if</code> and <code>else</code>.</p>\n<p><b>The basic idea:</b> \"if some condition is true, do this; otherwise, do that instead.\" In Python, this looks like:</p>\n<pre>if condition:\n    # runs only when the condition is True\nelse:\n    # runs only when the condition is False</pre>\n<p>A \"condition\" is just a question with a yes/no (True/False) answer, usually built using <b>comparison operators</b>:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tr><th>Operator</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><code>&gt;</code></td><td>greater than</td><td><code>n &gt; 0</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>&lt;</code></td><td>less than</td><td><code>n &lt; 100</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>&gt;=</code></td><td>greater than or equal to</td><td><code>marks &gt;= 40</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>&lt;=</code></td><td>less than or equal to</td><td><code>age &lt;= 18</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>==</code></td><td>equal to (note: two equals signs!)</td><td><code>n == 0</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>!=</code></td><td>not equal to</td><td><code>n != 0</code></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><b>Example A - checking if it's raining:</b></p>\n<pre>rainfall = 5\nif rainfall > 0:\n    print(\"Take an umbrella\")\nelse:\n    print(\"No umbrella needed\")</pre>\n<p>Since <code>rainfall</code> is 5, and <code>5 &gt; 0</code> is True, this prints <code>Take an umbrella</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Example B - checking voting eligibility:</b></p>\n<pre>age = 16\nif age >= 18:\n    print(\"Can vote\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Cannot vote yet\")</pre>\n<p>Since <code>16 &gt;= 18</code> is False, this prints <code>Cannot vote yet</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Now let's see the exact pattern you'll use for this question - checking if a number is positive:</b></p>\n<pre>n = int(input())\nif n > 0:\n    print(\"Positive\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Not Positive\")</pre>\n<p>Let's trace through this with three different inputs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If <code>n = 5</code>: <code>5 &gt; 0</code> is True, so we print <code>Positive</code>.</li>\n<li>If <code>n = -3</code>: <code>-3 &gt; 0</code> is False, so we skip the <code>if</code> block and print <code>Not Positive</code> instead.</li>\n<li>If <code>n = 0</code>: <code>0 &gt; 0</code> is False (zero is not greater than zero!), so we print <code>Not Positive</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>About indentation:</b> notice that the lines <code>print(\"Positive\")</code> and <code>print(\"Not Positive\")</code> both start with some blank space (usually 4 spaces) before the code begins - this is called <b>leading whitespace</b>, or indentation.</p>\n<p><b>This leading whitespace is not just for looks - it is how Python knows which lines belong inside the <code>if</code>, and which belong inside the <code>else</code>.</b> Get the indentation wrong or inconsistent, and your program will either misbehave or crash with an <code>IndentationError</code>. (Don't worry about extra spaces elsewhere on a line, like around a <code>+</code> or <code>&gt;</code> sign in the middle of a line - only the leading whitespace at the very start of a line matters.)</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Read a whole number. If it is greater than 0, print <code>Positive</code>. Otherwise (zero or negative), print <code>Not Positive</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Example 1:</b></p>\n<p>Input: <pre>5</pre> Output: <pre>Positive</pre></p>\n<p><b>Example 2:</b></p>\n<p>Input: <pre>-3</pre> Output: <pre>Not Positive</pre></p>\n<p><b>Example 3 (tricky edge case - zero):</b></p>\n<p>Input: <pre>0</pre> Output: <pre>Not Positive</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "5",
     "expected": "Positive",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "-3",
     "expected": "Not Positive",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "0",
     "expected": "Not Positive",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 11,
   "xml_name": "L02_011_Even Or Odd",
   "display_name": "Even Or Odd",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's use <code>if</code>-<code>else</code> together with something you already know: the <code>%</code> (modulus) operator, which gives the remainder after division.</p>\n<p>A number is <b>even</b> if it leaves no remainder when divided by 2 (i.e. <code>n % 2</code> equals <code>0</code>), and <b>odd</b> otherwise (the remainder is 1). This works for any whole number, even negative ones!</p>\n<pre>n = int(input())\nif n % 2 == 0:\n    print(\"Even\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Odd\")</pre>\n<p><b>Very important:</b> notice we use <code>==</code> (two equals signs) to <b>check</b> if two things are equal, inside the condition. This is completely different from a single <code>=</code>, which is used to <b>assign</b> a value to a variable, like <code>n = 4</code>. Mixing these up is one of the most common beginner mistakes!</p>\n<p><b>Let's trace through several examples:</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>n = 4</code>: <code>4 % 2</code> is <code>0</code>, and <code>0 == 0</code> is True, so we print <code>Even</code>.</li>\n<li><code>n = 7</code>: <code>7 % 2</code> is <code>1</code>, and <code>1 == 0</code> is False, so we print <code>Odd</code>.</li>\n<li><code>n = 10</code>: <code>10 % 2</code> is <code>0</code>, so we print <code>Even</code>.</li>\n<li><code>n = 0</code>: <code>0 % 2</code> is <code>0</code> (zero counts as even!), so we print <code>Even</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here's another everyday example of the same idea - deciding if a year is a \"leap year candidate\" (simplified, just checking divisibility by 4):</p>\n<pre>year = 2024\nif year % 4 == 0:\n    print(\"Possibly a leap year\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Not a leap year\")</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Read a whole number and print <code>Even</code> if it is even, or <code>Odd</code> if it is odd.</p>\n<p><b>Example 1:</b></p>\n<p>Input: <pre>4</pre> Output: <pre>Even</pre></p>\n<p><b>Example 2:</b></p>\n<p>Input: <pre>7</pre> Output: <pre>Odd</pre></p>\n<p><b>Example 3 (edge case - zero):</b></p>\n<p>Input: <pre>0</pre> Output: <pre>Even</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "4",
     "expected": "Even",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "7",
     "expected": "Odd",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "0",
     "expected": "Even",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 12,
   "xml_name": "L02_012_Pass Or Fail",
   "display_name": "Pass Or Fail",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's use <code>if</code>-<code>else</code> for a very common real-world scenario: deciding whether a student passes or fails an exam based on their marks.</p>\n<p>Suppose the passing threshold is 40 marks (out of 100). If a student scores 40 or more, they pass; otherwise, they fail. Notice this needs <code>&gt;=</code> (greater than <b>or equal to</b>), not just <code>&gt;</code>, because scoring exactly 40 should still count as a pass!</p>\n<pre>marks = int(input())\nif marks >= 40:\n    print(\"Pass\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Fail\")</pre>\n<p><b>Choosing the right comparison operator matters a lot here.</b> Compare these two versions:</p>\n<pre># Version 1: uses >= (correct for this problem)\nif marks >= 40:\n    print(\"Pass\")\n\n# Version 2: uses > (WRONG here - unfairly fails a student who scored exactly 40!)\nif marks > 40:\n    print(\"Pass\")</pre>\n<p><b>Let's trace through a few examples with the correct <code>&gt;=</code> version:</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>marks = 65</code>: <code>65 &gt;= 40</code> is True, so <code>Pass</code>.</li>\n<li><code>marks = 30</code>: <code>30 &gt;= 40</code> is False, so <code>Fail</code>.</li>\n<li><code>marks = 40</code>: <code>40 &gt;= 40</code> is True (equal counts!), so <code>Pass</code>.</li>\n<li><code>marks = 39</code>: <code>39 &gt;= 40</code> is False, so <code>Fail</code> - just one mark short!</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The same pattern appears everywhere - for example, checking if you're old enough to drive:</p>\n<pre>age = 18\nif age >= 18:\n    print(\"Eligible to drive\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Not eligible yet\")</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Read the marks scored by a student (a whole number). If the marks are 40 or more, print <code>Pass</code>. Otherwise, print <code>Fail</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Example 1:</b></p>\n<p>Input: <pre>65</pre> Output: <pre>Pass</pre></p>\n<p><b>Example 2:</b></p>\n<p>Input: <pre>30</pre> Output: <pre>Fail</pre></p>\n<p><b>Example 3 (edge case - exactly the threshold):</b></p>\n<p>Input: <pre>40</pre> Output: <pre>Pass</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "65",
     "expected": "Pass",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
